Let $S^1 = \{z \in \mathbb{C} : |z|=1\}$. For an integrable map $f : S^1 \to S^1 \subset \mathbb{C}$, write its Fourier coefficients as
$$a_n(f) = \frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{S^1} f(e^{i\theta}) e^{-in\theta}\,d\theta, \qquad n \in \mathbb{Z}.$$
For a sufficiently regular $f : S^1 \to S^1$ (so that the topological degree $\deg f \in \mathbb{Z}$ is defined), it is known that whenever $f, g : S^1 \to S^1$ satisfy
$$|a_n(f)| = |a_n(g)| \quad \text{for every } n \in \mathbb{Z},$$
one has $\deg f = \deg g$, provided either
\begin{itemize}
\item $f, g \in W^{1/3,3}(S^1; S^1)$ (the fractional Sobolev space with $s = 1/3$, $p = 3$), or
\item $f, g \in C^{0,\alpha}(S^1; S^1)$ for some Hölder exponent $\alpha > 1/3$.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Question.} Does the same implication
$$|a_n(f)| = |a_n(g)| \text{ for all } n \in \mathbb{Z} \;\Longrightarrow\; \deg f = \deg g$$
remain valid in the following rougher regularity classes?
\begin{enumerate}
\item $f, g \in W^{1/p,p}(S^1; S^1)$ with $p > 3$.
\item $f, g \in C^{0,\alpha}(S^1; S^1)$ with $\alpha \le 1/3$.
\end{enumerate}
The mixed-regularity versions are also unresolved: namely, the cases
\begin{itemize}
\item $f \in W^{1/p,p}$ with $2 < p \le 3$ and $g \in W^{1/q,q}$ with $q > 3$;
\item $f \in C^{0,\alpha}$ with $\alpha > 1/3$ and $g \in C^{0,\beta}$ with $\beta \le 1/3$.
\end{itemize}

Determine whether the equality $\deg f = \deg g$ continues to hold in each of these settings, or exhibit counterexamples.

